ral doc framing (line 3-4, both spec docs): both specs open with \"reverse-engineered spec\" / empirically verified against one pinned CLI build — the specs themselves flag that **turnbridge's whole fabrication approach rests on unofficial, version-pinned reverse engineering** that can silently break on CLI upgrades.\n\n## 2. TODO/FIXME/XXX/HACK and stub search in src/\n\n- **No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, or `HACK` comments found anywhere in `src/`** (`grep -rn -E \"TODO|FIXME|XXX|HACK\" src` → zero matches).\n- **No \"not implemented\" / \"unimplemented\" / \"stub\" / \"unsupported\" language found** in src (only doc-comment mentions of the `FabricationUnsupportedError` class itself, which is a real implemented control-flow mechanism, not a stub).\n- Only `throw new Error` in src is a test helper: `src/test/helpers.ts:22` — `if (!repo) throw new Error(\"failed to init temp repo\");` (test scaffolding assertion, not a product gap).\n- `FabricationUnsupportedError` is thrown in exactly two guarded spots, both are \"version couldn't be detected\" cases, not missing functionality:\n  - `src/targets/claude-code.ts:201` — thrown when `claude --version` can't be parsed.\n  - `src/targets/codex.ts:160` — thrown when `codex --version` can't be parsed.\n- No skipped/disabled tests (`.skip`/`.todo`/`xit`/`xdescribe`) found in `src/test/`.\n- Test coverage by file (rough, from `test(` counts): `claude-fabricate.test.ts` (2), `conversations.test.ts` (2), `transcript.test.ts` (1), `lineage.test.ts` (2), `codex-fabricate.test.ts` (1) — thin test suite overall (8 top-level `test(` calls across 5 files); no tests for `cli.ts`, `resume.ts` (picker/target-selection flow), `shim.ts`, `launch.ts`, `config.ts`, `bootstrap.ts` prompt generation. This is an inventory gap even though it's not marked with a comment.\n\nNet: the codebase has **zero explicit inline markers of pending work**; all \"pending work\" signal lives in the docs (design doc \"Risks\" section, spec docs' \"Unknowns\" sections) rather than code comments — i.e., the gaps are architectural/validation gaps, not unfinished code paths.\n\n## 3. Architecture summary (~10 sentences)\n\nTurnbridge is a CLI (`turnbridge`, alias `tb`) that lets a developer resume a coding-agent conversation in a *different* CLI than the one it originated in, without claiming to transfer hidden model state. It supports exactly two target CLIs today: Claude Code and Codex (`src/types.ts:4-6`, `SUPPORTED_CLIS`), with a `TargetAdapter` interface (`src/targets/types.ts`) designed so more CLIs can be added later by implementing `isInstalled`/`nativeResume`/`fabricate`/`bootstrap`. \"Fabrication\" means turnbridge writes a brand-new, real native session file in the target CLI's own on-disk format (`~/.claude/projects/&lt;encoded-cwd&gt;/&lt;uuid&gt;.jsonl` for Claude Code, `~/.codex/sessions/&lt;date&gt;/rollout-*.jsonl` for Codex) containing the source conversation's turns translated into that target's schema, then hands off to the target's own native `--resume`/`resume` command — the target CLI itself believes it's resuming a session it wrote (`src/targets/claude-code.ts:189-230`, `src/targets/codex.ts:148-190`). When fabrication is unsupported (unknown/unvalidated installed CLI version) or the user passes `--bootstrap`, turnbridge instead falls back to \"bootstrap rehydration\": writing a literal markdown transcript to disk and launching a brand-new target session whose first prompt instructs the model to read that file (`src/bootstrap.ts`, `src/transcript.ts`). Turnbridge is a pure client of the sibling package `conversation-ledger` (imported by path as `conversation-ledger`, currently linked via `npm install` from `../conversation-ledger`, package version `0.9.0`): it never captures or parses source CLI transcripts itself — that's entirely the ledger's job via its own Claude Code/Codex capture adapters and hooks (`cledger install all`). From `conversation-ledger` turnbridge imports and calls: `readEvents` (to pull `conversation_turn` events, filtered by `kind` and `reachableFrom: \"HEAD\"`, in `src/conversations.ts:40-43` and `continuation`-kind events in `src/lineage.ts:70-73`), `sortEvents` (`src/conversations.ts:55`), `appendEvents` (to write its own `continuation` lineage events back into the ledger, `src/lineage.ts:35-51`), `findRepo` and `gitUserIdentity` (`src/resume.ts:1,147,152`), plus the `EvidenceEvent` and `RepoInfo` types throughout. Turnbridge writes exactly one kind of ledger event itself, `continuation` (`src/lineage.ts:3`), recording a `links: [{rel:\"continues\", target:&lt;source&gt;}]` edge each time it fabricates a cross-CLI session, so the picker can show \"→ bridged to X\" / \"↳ continued from Y\" annotations (`src/resume.ts:33-43`) — it never writes conversation content events. The main flow (`resumeCommand` in `src/resume.ts`) is: find the git repo, read the user's git identity, list/filter conversations by ownership and commit-reachability, let the user pick a conversation and a target CLI (interactively or via flags/config default), then build and run a `LaunchPlan` (same-CLI → native resume; cross-CLI → fabricate-then-fallback-to-bootstrap). An opt-in shim system (`src/shim.ts`) can rewrite `~/.zshrc` PATH to intercept bare `claude --resume`/`codex resume` invocations and redirect them into turnbridge's merged picker while passing everything else through untouched.\n\n## 4. Version assumptions about conversation-ledger baked into turnbridge\n\n- **Event kinds turnbridge reads/writes** (hard-coded string literals, not sourced from any shared enum in conversation-ledger):\n  - Reads `kind: \"conversation_turn\"` — `src/conversations.ts:41`.\n  - Reads/writes `kind: \"continuation\"` — `src/lineage.ts:3` (`CONTINUATION_KIND`), used in `readEvents(..., {kind: CONTINUATION_KIND})` (`src/lineage.ts:71`) and `appendEvents` (`src/lineage.ts:37`).\n  - Uses `links` rel value `\"continues\"` — `src/lineage.ts:4` (`CONTINUES_REL`), hard-coded string, matched against ledger's generic `links: EventLink[]` extension point (`conversation-ledger/src/schema.ts:92`).\n- **EvidenceEvent field shape assumptions** (from `conversation-ledger/src/schema.ts:74-108`, currently matching): `id`, `kind`, `occurred_at`, `actor {type, id, display}`, `producer {source, session_id, tool, version}`, `content`, `conversation {id, seq}`, `links`. Turnbridge assumes `actor.type` is one of `\"human\" | \"agent\" | \"system\"` (`src/conversations.ts:59`, `src/lineage.ts:39`, `src/targets/claude-code.ts:137,139`, `src/targets/codex.ts:134`) — this enum is not re-exported/typed from conversation-ledger's public API in what was read; it's an assumed convention.\n- **`readEvents` options API**: assumes support for `{kind, reachableFrom: \"HEAD\"}` filter shape (`src/conversations.ts:40-43`, `src/lineage.ts:70-73`) — matches `ReadOptions` type exported from conversation-ledger's `store.js`.\n- **`appendEvents` accepts an array of event drafts** with `kind`, `occurred_at`, `actor`, `producer`, `conversation`, `links`, `content` — assumes `conversation: {id, seq: 0}` is valid for a non-`conversation_turn` event type riding under an existing conversation id (`src/lineage.ts:36-51`); this is an implicit assumption about how conversation-ledger scopes/dedupes events by `conversation.id` for non-turn event kinds, not explicitly documented as tested in the repo's docs.\n- **`producer.source` / `producer.session_id` convention**: turnbridge derives CLI name and native session id from `producer.source` and `producer.session_id`, falling back to parsing the conversation id string (`id.split(\":\")`) when absent (`src/conversations.ts:66-67`) — implies an assumed id-namespacing convention (`&lt;source&gt;:&lt;session-id&gt;`) that isn't verified against conversation-ledger's actual id-generation code in this pass.\n- **Identity stamping is versioned/optional**: `docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:89-91` explicitly notes \"Events captured before identity stamping existed are unattributed; a forced transcript rescan after the upgrade can duplicate those turns under new event ids\" — a direct backward-compatibility assumption about older conversation-ledger-captured data.\n- **CLI-version pins are turnbridge's own, independent of conversation-ledger's version**, but gate fabrication safety: `VALIDATED_VERSION_PREFIX = \"2.\"` for Claude Code (`src/targets/claude-code.ts:17`) and `VALIDATED_VERSION_PREFIX = \"0.144.\"` for Codex (`src/targets/codex.ts:16`) — both are prefix checks against the *target CLI's* own version, not conversation-ledger's, but they're the operative \"version assumption baked in\" mechanism described in the WIP doc's \"Fabrication contract\" section (`docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:64-67`).\n- No explicit check anywhere in `src/` of conversation-ledger's own package `version` or a `SCHEMA_VERSION`/compatibility field at runtime — turnbridge's `package.json:33` pins the dependency only via `\"conversation-ledger\": \"file:../conversation-ledger\"` (a local path dependency, not a semver range), meaning compatibility is enforced only by co-development in this monorepo-like sibling-directory setup, with no runtime guard against a conversation-ledger schema change breaking turnbridge silently.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>67475</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>30</tool_uses><duration_ms>122317</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>","type":"text"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"29a543fb82805e7dc0998709190735a9e002153e","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","seq":35},"id":"ev1-67c51ae05208415dd9cff17c589feae70b85f579883bada440e74ace49fb82f9","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-21T21:23:03.042Z","producer":{"session_id":"6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","source":"claude-code","tool":"cledger","version":"0.9.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"